<section class="block alt" id="japan">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2 data-i18n="jp_h2">Why Japan</h2>
    <div class="why">
      <div><h3 data-i18n="jp1_t">Robots are built from Japanese parts</h3><p data-i18n="jp1_d">Many of the reducers, motors and sensors inside today's robots come from Japan. The industry that makes them is here.</p></div>
      <div><h3 data-i18n="jp2_t">Data that has to stay here</h3><p data-i18n="jp2_d">Japanese companies are cautious about sending factory and workplace video abroad. Tools that run locally, with local support, fit how they work.</p></div>
      <div><h3 data-i18n="jp3_t">Robots will work in Japanese settings</h3><p data-i18n="jp3_d">Robots sold in Japan need data from Japanese workplaces, tools and language. Today, most training data comes from somewhere else.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="block" id="about">
  <div class="wrap about">
    <h2 data-i18n="ab_h2">About HUMI</h2>
    <div>
      <p data-i18n="ab_p1">HUMI (Humanoid Industries) is an independent company in Fukuoka, Japan. It was founded by an engineer from semiconductor manufacturing and factory automation, who has worked on production lines and machine vision.</p>
      <p data-i18n="ab_p2">We build practical tools for the people collecting and using robot training data, and we publish what we learn about Japan's robotics industry. We work in Japanese, English and French.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

